---
scope: src/cli/init
status: aligned
description: >
  Fast cold-start installer that writes every canonical AIDE artifact it can
  safely place without user interaction, never overwrites existing files
  (and never touches anything under the user-owned `.aide/config/` directory
  on subsequent runs), and surfaces a prominent terminal warning naming the
  right follow-up surface per category (orchestrator inline-recovery via
  /aide for vault path and brain wiring, re-running the cli with --ide for
  IDE choice, /aide:upgrade for drift in shipped artifacts) for anything it
  had to skip or defer.
intent: >
  The CLI bootstrapper is the sole cold-start installer — the one
  `npx` invocation that turns a cold project into an AIDE-ready
  project end-to-end in the common case, without any Claude Code
  session yet. It runs in a plain terminal before any agent exists,
  so it has no user to interview: the only safe write policy is
  create-if-missing, skip-if-present, with the user-owned
  `.aide/config/` directory carrying an even stronger guarantee —
  files inside are scaffolded once on first cold start if absent,
  and the CLI never touches them again on any subsequent run.
  There are no confirmation prompts. The only flags the CLI accepts
  are `--help` and a small, explicitly-sanctioned set of escape
  hatches whose sole purpose is to supply, up-front, the answers to
  the inherently-interactive decisions the CLI would otherwise have
  to defer — currently `--vault-path <path>` (brain vault) and
  `--ide <choice>` (IDE configuration). Running `npx` is itself the
  opt-in.

  The CLI installs everything in the non-interactive footprint:
  the methodology pointer stub, the methodology doc hub under
  `.aide/docs/`, the full set of pipeline slash commands, the full
  set of pipeline agent definitions, the full set of skill
  templates, the `aide` MCP server entry (additively merged into
  `.mcp.json`), and the `aide-tree` launcher script. When the user
  supplies `--vault-path`, the CLI also scaffolds the canonical
  Obsidian `.aide/config/brain.aide` (frontmatter `name` plus
  `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`, defaulting to a
  `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` launcher with the supplied vault path
  carried inline byte-for-byte in `args`) and additively merges the
  derived `brain` MCP server entry into `.mcp.json` — the
  scaffolded file is the source of truth for the launcher shape,
  not a constant inlined in the CLI. For every package-owned file
  it writes, it checks disk first — if the file exists, the CLI
  skips it and records the skip. For every file under
  `.aide/config/`, the CLI checks disk first AND, once written,
  the bytes belong to the user forever — the file is scaffolded
  once on a cold start where it is absent, and never touched again
  on any subsequent run regardless of drift from the canonical
  default. The CLI never overwrites either class.

  Categories that depend on inherent user decisions are deferred
  to the right interactive surface unless the user supplied the
  corresponding flag at invocation time. When `--vault-path` is
  absent, `.aide/config/brain.aide` is not scaffolded (the CLI
  cannot author the user-owned file without knowing the vault
  path) and the brain MCP entry is not written either (it is
  derived from that file at sync time) — the two collapse into
  one deferred decision because the user owns one file and sync
  derives the entry. The deferred recovery route is: open Claude
  Code and run `/aide`; the orchestrator prompts for the vault
  path, scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide`, and tells you to run
  `npx aidemd-mcp sync`. Sync reads the scaffolded
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` and writes the brain MCP entry into
  `.mcp.json`. When `--vault-path` is supplied, the CLI resolves
  both halves in order: writes `.aide/config/brain.aide`, derives
  the MCP entry from it, and merges the entry into `.mcp.json`.
  Post-install edits to `.aide/config/brain.aide` (retargeting
  the vault path inline in `mcpServerConfig.args`, swapping the
  launcher command, rewriting the prose body) propagate via
  `npx aidemd-mcp sync` — not by re-running init, which never
  touches the user-owned file again. IDE configuration is
  deferred to a re-run of the CLI with `--ide` when the flag is
  absent, and applied when it is supplied.

  After the install pass, the CLI emits a prominent, hard-to-miss
  terminal warning — a loud ASCII-bordered block, not a routine
  log line — listing every file it skipped and every category it
  deferred, with each entry naming the right follow-up surface for
  that category: deferred brain wiring (vault path plus the
  derived MCP entry, as one combined item) points the user at
  opening Claude Code and running `/aide` (the orchestrator's
  inline-recovery flow prompts for the path, scaffolds
  `.aide/config/brain.aide`, and directs the user to run sync);
  deferred IDE configuration points at re-running the CLI with
  `--ide <choice>`; skipped shipped artifacts (already-present
  canonical files that may have drifted from the package's
  current canonical content) point at `/aide:upgrade` for guided
  reconciliation. When the CLI finishes with nothing skipped and
  nothing deferred (a fully installed project or a clean cold
  start with no inherent-decision residue), the warning is
  omitted and a plain "already set up" or "install complete"
  line is printed instead.

  The CLI must be safe to run repeatedly, must never destroy
  existing MCP entries or other files, must abort without writing
  if `.mcp.json` is malformed, must never mutate any file under
  `.aide/config/` after its first-write scaffold, and must always
  leave the project in a state where the user can finish the job
  for anything the CLI could not do itself by following the
  per-category follow-up surface named in the warning.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - "Running the CLI installs every canonical artifact that has no inherent user decision attached: the methodology pointer stub, the methodology doc hub under `.aide/docs/`, every pipeline slash command, every pipeline agent definition, every skill template, the `aide` MCP server entry (additively merged into `.mcp.json`), and the `aide-tree` launcher script at `.aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs`. When `--vault-path` is supplied, the CLI additionally scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` from the canonical Obsidian template and additively merges the derived `brain` MCP server entry into `.mcp.json` under the fixed `brain` key. When `--vault-path` is absent, both brain artifacts are omitted from this run and surfaced as a single deferred item in the warning. The CLI is the sole cold-start surface that scaffolds these canonical artifacts; no slash command duplicates this responsibility."
    - "For every package-owned file it writes, the CLI checks disk first and never overwrites. If the target file exists, the CLI skips it and records the skip for the final warning, with the warning naming `/aide:upgrade` as the follow-up surface for reconciling drift in shipped artifacts. Files under the user-owned `.aide/config/` directory (currently `.aide/config/brain.aide`) carry a stronger guarantee: scaffolded once on a cold start where the file is absent, and never touched again on any subsequent run regardless of byte drift from the canonical default — the directory path itself is the ownership signal, with no per-file allowlist. Skip-on-exists is the only safe branch for shipped artifacts; presence-once-then-hands-off is the only safe branch for `.aide/config/`. The CLI has no user to consent to either form of overwrite, and reconciliation against the package's current canonical content for shipped artifacts is `/aide:upgrade`'s job; reconciliation of `.aide/config/` content is the user's via direct edits plus `npx aidemd-mcp sync`."
    - "The `aide` MCP server entry is always additively merged into `.mcp.json` — if the file does not exist it is created with just the `aide` entry; if it exists with other server entries (`github`, custom servers, etc.) every existing entry is preserved and only `aide` is added. The key is `\"aide\"` (matching the dual-key check `\"aide\" in servers || \"aidemd-mcp\" in servers`)."
    - "If `.mcp.json` exists but contains malformed JSON, the CLI prints a clear error naming the file, exits non-zero, and writes nothing — it never clobbers a broken config."
    - "Categories that require an inherent user decision — brain wiring (vault path plus the derived MCP entry, treated as one decision because both descend from the user-owned `.aide/config/brain.aide`) and IDE configuration choice — are deferred to a category-specific follow-up surface only when the corresponding escape-hatch flag (`--vault-path`, `--ide`) was not supplied. When the user supplies `--vault-path <path>`, the CLI uses that path as the resolved vault location and writes it inline byte-for-byte into the scaffolded `brain.aide`'s `mcpServerConfig.args`; when the user supplies `--ide <choice>`, the CLI applies the IDE configuration for that choice. When the flag is absent, the category is added to the deferred list in the final warning with the right follow-up named: brain wiring defers to opening Claude Code and running `/aide` (the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow detects the missing `.aide/config/brain.aide` via the boot reporter's `no-brain-aide` state, prompts for the vault path, scaffolds the file, and directs the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp sync`); IDE configuration defers to re-running the CLI with `--ide <choice>`. The CLI never guesses, defaults, or scans the filesystem to fill these decisions."
    - "When (and only when) `--vault-path` is supplied, the CLI writes the `brain` MCP server entry into `.mcp.json` under the fixed `brain` key. The entry is derived from the just-scaffolded `.aide/config/brain.aide` — its `command` and `args` come straight out of the file's `mcpServerConfig`, with the canonical default producing a `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` launcher and the supplied vault path inlined byte-for-byte in `args`. The merge into `.mcp.json` is purely additive: every existing MCP server entry survives untouched. When `--vault-path` is absent the CLI does not write a `brain` MCP entry at all (no placeholder, no empty path slot, no synthesized stub), and the boot reporter inside Claude Code detects the missing `.aide/config/brain.aide` via the `no-brain-aide` state — the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow then prompts for the vault path, scaffolds the file, and directs the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp sync` which writes the entry."
    - "Recognized flags are `--help`, `--vault-path <path>`, and `--ide <choice>`. Each non-help flag exists for exactly one reason: to let the user supply, up-front, the answer to an inherently-interactive decision the CLI would otherwise defer to its category-specific follow-up surface (brain wiring → orchestrator inline recovery via `/aide`; IDE → re-running the CLI with `--ide`). New flags may be added only when they meet the same bar — they bypass an interactive deferral that would otherwise land in the warning. Flags that gate behavior unrelated to inherent decisions (verbosity, dry-run, force, framework selection) are out of scope for the cold-start CLI."
    - "After the install pass, the CLI prints a prominent, hard-to-miss terminal warning block — loud ASCII borders, uppercase heading, clearly separated from the per-file log lines — that names every file it skipped (because it already existed) and every category it deferred (brain wiring as one combined item when `--vault-path` was not supplied, IDE when `--ide` was not supplied), and tells the user the right follow-up surface per category: deferred brain wiring → open Claude Code and run `/aide` (the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow prompts for the vault path, scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide`, and directs the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp sync`); deferred IDE → re-run the CLI with `--ide <choice>`; skipped shipped artifacts → run `/aide:upgrade` for guided reconciliation."
    - "When the install pass has nothing to warn about — every file the CLI would have written already existed and there are no inherent-decision categories to defer in the current run — the warning block is omitted and a plain `Already set up.` or `Install complete.` line is printed instead, with no follow-up surface named because there is nothing left to follow up on."
    - "Per-file terminal output is terse and scannable: one line per artifact with a `[created]` or `[exists]` status prefix. No colors, no spinners, no progress bars, no ASCII art in the per-file log — the loud ASCII is reserved for the final warning so it stands out."
    - "Running the CLI is idempotent. A second run on a project where the CLI already completed its install writes no files, reports every artifact as `[exists]`, and either prints the `Already set up.` line (if no categories remain deferred) or re-prints the same deferred-category warning as the first run."
    - "The CLI completes its default happy path with no user interaction beyond running `npx` itself — no confirmation prompts, no `y/N` gates, no in-run input requests. Flags are accepted only when they were supplied on the invocation line, and even then they must not block on user input mid-run."
    - "Exit code is 0 when the install pass completes without error, regardless of whether files were skipped or categories deferred. Exit code is non-zero only for actual failures (malformed `.mcp.json`, unwritable filesystem, missing canonical content)."
    - "After the CLI completes, the per-category follow-up surfaces named in the warning are sufficient — and individually executable — to finish the setup for every skipped file and every deferred category. Brain wiring is finished by opening Claude Code and running `/aide` (the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow prompts for the vault path, scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide`, and tells you to run `npx aidemd-mcp sync`, which reads the file and writes the derived `brain` MCP entry into `.mcp.json`); IDE is finished by re-running the CLI with `--ide <choice>`; skipped shipped artifacts are reconciled by running `/aide:upgrade`. No single surface owns the whole follow-up, and the user is never told to run a removed slash command."
    - "When `--vault-path` is supplied, the CLI scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` using the canonical Obsidian template (frontmatter `name` plus `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`, defaulting to a `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` launcher with the supplied vault path inlined byte-for-byte in `args`) before computing the brain MCP entry. The scaffolded file lives inside the user-owned `.aide/config/` directory and inherits its ownership semantics: scaffolded once on a cold start where it is absent, then belongs to the user forever — the CLI never patches, migrates, or rewrites it on any subsequent run regardless of byte drift from the canonical default. The brain MCP entry written into `.mcp.json` is derived from the scaffolded (or pre-existing) `.aide/config/brain.aide` via `parseBrainAideFromString` + `interpolateArgs` — never from a hardcoded launcher constant. When `--vault-path` is absent, both `.aide/config/brain.aide` and the brain MCP entry are deferred together as a single inherent decision."
    - "Post-install brain edits (retargeting the vault path inline in `mcpServerConfig.args`, swapping the launcher command, renaming the brain via the `name` field, rewriting the prose body) propagate to `.mcp.json` via `npx aidemd-mcp sync` — not by re-running `init`, which never touches the user-owned `.aide/config/brain.aide` after its first-write scaffold. The warning block names sync as the follow-up verb for deferred brain wiring, so users who want to do everything from the terminal have a clear path."
  undesired:
    - "The CLI overwrites an existing file because it assumed the on-disk copy was stale or out of date. The CLI has no user to consent to an overwrite — skip-on-exists is the only safe branch, and any reconciliation against the package's current canonical content must be deferred to `/aide:upgrade`. The same prohibition applies with extra force to anything under `.aide/config/`: even if the on-disk content has drifted from the canonical default, even if a newer template would be more correct, the CLI must not patch, migrate, or rewrite a file inside that directory after the first-write scaffold. New defaults reach existing hosts only through `npx aidemd-mcp sync` (for the derived `.mcp.json` entry) or a deliberate user edit (for the file itself), never through the install path."
    - "The CLI finishes with files skipped or categories deferred but prints no warning, leaving the user to discover their install is incomplete only when they later trip over a missing agent, missing command, or missing MCP entry."
    - "The warning about skipped files and deferred categories is quiet, inline with the routine `[created]/[exists]` log lines, or formatted in a way the user can miss when skimming. The warning must be unmissable — loud ASCII borders, clear separation, and explicit per-category follow-up instructions naming the right surface for each (open Claude Code and run `/aide` for brain wiring, re-run the CLI with `--ide <choice>` for IDE, run `/aide:upgrade` for skipped shipped artifacts)."
    - "The warning block is printed even when there is nothing to warn about. A clean install with no skips and no deferrals must print a plain completion line, not a loud box with an empty body."
    - "The CLI attempts brain vault provisioning by guessing a path (sibling folder, environment variable, platform default) without a user to confirm. Vault location is an inherent decision and always belongs to the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow inside Claude Code (the user opens Claude and runs `/aide`, which detects the missing path via `aide_info` and prompts for it)."
    - "The CLI writes the `brain` MCP server entry on a run where `--vault-path` was not supplied — synthesizing a stub, a placeholder, an empty path slot, or any pre-wired entry the user did not consent to. The brain MCP entry is derived from the user-owned `.aide/config/brain.aide`; without that file present, the entry has no source-of-truth to derive from and must not be written. The boot reporter's `no-brain-aide` state, not a placeholder MCP entry, is what routes the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow when the user opens Claude Code and runs `/aide`. Writing a placeholder entry would reintroduce a package-owned launcher constant the refactor explicitly retired and contradict the user-ownership invariant that everything under `.aide/config/` carries."
    - "The CLI invents or guesses a vault path to scaffold `.aide/config/brain.aide` (and the derived `brain` entry's `mcpServerConfig.args`) when `--vault-path` was not supplied. Without the flag, the CLI must NOT scaffold the file at all — neither a placeholder, a sibling-folder guess, an env-var read, nor a platform default. Brain wiring is deferred as a single inherent decision and surfaced in the warning. Guessing here is the same failure mode as guessing brain provisioning — the CLI has no user to confirm and must surface the gap, not paper over it."
    - "The CLI attempts IDE configuration (Zed, VS Code extension install, file-association edits) on a run where `--ide` was not supplied. IDE choice is an inherent decision; without the flag, the only correct path is to defer the category and tell the user to re-run the CLI with `--ide <choice>`."
    - "Overwriting or removing existing MCP server entries that belong to other tools when merging the `aide` entry into an existing config. The merge must be purely additive."
    - "Silently succeeding while writing malformed JSON to `.mcp.json`, leaving the project in a state where no MCP servers load."
    - "Clobbering an existing `.mcp.json` that contains malformed JSON. The CLI must detect the parse failure, print a clear error naming the file, exit non-zero, and leave the file untouched."
    - "Writing the aide MCP entry under a key other than `\"aide\"` (for example `\"aidemd-mcp-server\"` or `\"aide-server\"`). The dual-key check `\"aide\" in servers || \"aidemd-mcp\" in servers` is the contract; any other key causes downstream tooling (including `/aide:upgrade` and the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow) to see no existing entry and offer to add a duplicate."
    - "Prompting the user interactively on the happy path — `y/N` gates, conversational questions, or any in-run input request. The CLI is opt-in by virtue of the user running `npx`; further confirmation adds friction to a non-interactive bootstrap."
    - "Adding flags that gate behavior unrelated to bypassing an inherent-decision deferral — verbosity toggles, dry-run, force-overwrite, framework selection, dotfile discovery, network opt-in, telemetry. The non-help flag surface is reserved exclusively for supplying the answers to interactive decisions the CLI would otherwise have to defer to a category-specific follow-up surface (brain wiring → orchestrator inline recovery via `/aide`; IDE → re-running the CLI with `--ide`). Anything else is scope creep that drags the cold-start CLI toward a general-purpose configuration tool it must not become."
    - "The CLI aborts the whole install on the first per-file failure. An unwritable agent file must not prevent the methodology stub, the slash commands, the MCP merge, and the launcher from being installed — failed writes go into the final warning alongside skipped files, and the CLI continues."
    - "The CLI reaches for the network. Every artifact it writes is checked into the package; an offline machine must get the same result as an online one."
    - "The terminal warning, the per-file log, the `--help` output, or any other CLI-emitted text directs the user to a removed or non-existent slash command (e.g., `/aide:init`). Every follow-up surface named in CLI output must resolve to a currently-shipped surface: `/aide` (the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow handles missing vault path), re-running the CLI itself with `--ide <choice>` for IDE configuration, or `/aide:upgrade` for reconciling skipped shipped artifacts. Naming a removed surface looks helpful but leaves the user chasing a command that no longer exists."
    - "The CLI computes the brain MCP entry by calling a hardcoded launcher constant (such as `obsidianMcpEntry()`, a `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`-shaped object literal, or any in-code launcher table) instead of deriving it from the scaffolded `.aide/config/brain.aide` via `parseBrainAideFromString` + `interpolateArgs`. The scaffolded file is the canonical source of truth for the launcher shape; a hardcoded constant bypasses user ownership and contradicts the `.aide/config/brain.aide`-as-source-of-truth invariant the parent spec establishes. The same prohibition applies to introspecting `mcpServerConfig.args` to extract a vault path, statting it for validation, or branching on connector identity inferred from the args shape — path validation is the launcher's job at MCP server startup, not the CLI's."
    - "The CLI writes `.mcp.json` (or mutates any MCP entry) through any path other than the shared `writeMcpEntry` helper. All `.mcp.json` mutations in the CLI — `aide` entry, `brain` entry, legacy `obsidian` key removal — must flow through the shared helper to guarantee uniform key-preservation semantics and the abort-on-malformed-JSON contract."
    - "The CLI scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` with a frontmatter shape that drifts from the canonical schema — adding back retired fields (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools`), borrowing intent-spec frontmatter conventions (`scope`, `intent`, `outcomes`, `status`), or omitting either of the two load-bearing fields (`name`, `mcpServerConfig`). The scaffolded file's frontmatter must contain exactly `name` and `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` and nothing else; the brain's interface contract lives in the prose body, not in frontmatter declarations the runtime never consumes."
    - "The CLI scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` with a launcher other than the canonical `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` default, hardcodes the launcher bytes anywhere outside `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`, or leaves a literal `${rootPath}` (or any other unsubstituted placeholder) in the scaffolded file's `mcpServerConfig.args`. The default scaffold carries the supplied `--vault-path` value inline byte-for-byte in `args`, with no install-time interpolation surface required for the default case."
---

## Context

Every AIDE adoption runs into the same chicken-and-egg: the interactive bootstrap tool that would normally set up a project (`aide_init`) lives behind the MCP server, and the MCP server cannot be invoked until it has been registered in the project's MCP config. Before any agent session can help, something else must place those first files — and that something has to run in a plain terminal, before any Claude Code session, with no agent available to interview the user. The CLI bootstrapper is that something.

The prior shape of this CLI installed only a three-artifact minimum: the `aide` MCP entry, an initial command stub, and the `aide-tree` launcher. On paper that was enough to unblock the interactive bootstrap; in practice it was the wrong contract. A user who ran `npx` and got three lines of output reasonably concluded their project was ready to use — then hit a missing methodology stub, a missing pipeline slash command, or a missing agent file the first time they actually invoked a pipeline phase, and had no way to tell whether that was a bug, an incomplete install, or a step they forgot. The CLI had quietly delegated the rest of the install to a follow-up surface but said nothing about it, so users discovered the gap by tripping on it. The sister intent shift in `src/tools/init` made this worse: `aide_init` now runs silently through any category whose steps are all creates, which means the user can run the full chain and never see the artifacts they are missing.

The frontmatter's new contract repositions the CLI as a **cold-start installer**, not a minimum bootstrap. Everything the CLI can safely write without a user to consult — every canonical artifact whose content is pure methodology with no user decision attached — gets installed on the `npx` run. Only the categories that truly require an interview (brain wiring, which combines the vault path, the dependent `.aide/config/brain.aide` scaffold, and the brain MCP entry derived from it into a single user-owned decision; plus IDE choice) are deferred to per-category follow-up surfaces: brain wiring defers to the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow (open Claude Code and run `/aide`; the orchestrator prompts for the vault path, scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide`, and tells you to run `npx aidemd-mcp sync`); IDE defers to re-running the CLI with `--ide <choice>`; drift in shipped artifacts defers to `/aide:upgrade`. The root revision that landed `.aide/config/` as a user-owned directory tightened this further: the CLI scaffolds files inside `.aide/config/` once if absent and never on subsequent runs, regardless of byte drift from the canonical default — the directory path itself is the ownership signal, not a per-file allowlist. And because the CLI cannot prompt mid-run, its only safe branches remain skip or abort: it never overwrites, it never patches user-owned config, it aborts without writing on a malformed `.mcp.json`, and anything it skipped or deferred is surfaced in a terminal warning loud enough that a user cannot walk away thinking the install is finished when it is not.

## Strategy

**The canonical-artifact matrix splits by decision, not by file type.** Every category in `src/service/install` falls into one of two bins. CLI-installable (pure canonical content, zero inherent decisions): the methodology pointer stub, the methodology doc hub under `.aide/docs/`, every pipeline slash command enumerated in `scaffoldCommands/index.ts`' `COMMANDS` registry, every pipeline agent enumerated in `initContent/index.ts`' `AGENT_DOCS`, every skill template enumerated in `SKILL_DOCS`, the `aide` MCP entry (additively merged), and the `aide-tree` launcher. When `--vault-path` is supplied, `.aide/config/brain.aide` and the derived brain MCP entry also land in this bin: the CLI writes `.aide/config/brain.aide` first (from `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`, which produces the canonical Obsidian default with the `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` launcher and the supplied vault path inlined byte-for-byte in `mcpServerConfig.args`), then derives and merges the brain entry via `parseBrainAideFromString` + `interpolateArgs`. Deferred to per-category follow-up surfaces (inherent decisions only a user can resolve): when `--vault-path` is absent, brain wiring defers as one combined item (the user-owned `.aide/config/brain.aide` and the derived MCP entry are two halves of one decision) — the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow handles it (open Claude Code and run `/aide`; the orchestrator prompts for the vault path, scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide`, and tells you to run `npx aidemd-mcp sync`); IDE choice routes to re-running the CLI with `--ide <choice>`. The CLI never attempts to resolve these inherent decisions — guessing a brain path from a sibling folder or an env var is exactly the undesired behavior the frontmatter calls out. The split mirrors the rule the sister spec codifies: prompts exist for overwrites and for inherent decisions, and since the CLI cannot prompt, it can only own the non-inherent, no-overwrite surface.

**Reuse the imperative write paths from each `src/service/install/*` category; do not pull the `InitStep` planning machinery.** The existing `installMethodologyDocs`, `installAgents`, `installSkills`, and `scaffoldCommands` helpers are planners — they return `InitStep[]` for an agent-driven `applySteps` pass. The CLI is imperative: read state, decide, write, print, continue. The cleanest reuse is the leaf-level primitives those planners depend on — `readCanonicalDoc` for content resolution, `listMethodologyDocs`/`listAgents`/`listSkills`/`COMMANDS` for the canonical enumerations, `mcpEntry()` and the dual-key check from `wireMcp`, and `composeStub`/`getMethodologyMarker` from `writeMethodology` for the pointer stub. A thin per-artifact helper in `src/cli/init/` (modeled on the existing `writeMcpEntry`, `writeInitCommand`, `writeAideTree`) wraps each reused primitive in a stat-then-write-or-skip flow and returns a `{ status, message }` result the orchestrator can print in one line. Importing the planners themselves would drag in `InitStep` typing, `applySteps` batching, and the `would-*` status vocabulary — all of which exist so the MCP tool can hand off decisions to an agent, and none of which the CLI needs.

**Skip-on-exists is the only safe branch, per-file — and `.aide/config/` carries an even stronger guarantee.** Every package-owned artifact follows the same three-step shape: resolve the absolute host path (joining `projectRoot` with the canonical relative path), `fs.access` the path, and if present record a skip with a stable reason string and move on. If absent, create the parent directory tree, read the canonical content via `readCanonicalDoc`, write the file, and record a create. The frontmatter's `outcomes.desired` "never overwrites" contract is the governing constraint: the CLI has no user to consent to an overwrite, so the only safe branch is skip. Files inside `.aide/config/` (currently `.aide/config/brain.aide`) inherit the same skip-on-exists shape from their first scaffold but additionally fall under the parent intent's user-owned directory invariant: once written, the bytes belong to the user forever and the CLI must not patch, migrate, or rewrite them on any subsequent run regardless of byte drift from the canonical default. The directory path is the ownership signal — no per-file allowlist — so any new user-owned config file added later inherits the same semantics automatically. This matches the shape `writeInitCommand` and `writeAideTree` already use and extends it across the full artifact set with the user-owned directory carved out as untouchable after first write. Any artifact whose canonical read fails (e.g., `readCanonicalDoc` throws because the package is partially unpacked) records a skip with the error message as its reason — it must not crash the whole run.

**MCP merge is read-parse-merge-write with dual-key existence check and abort-on-parse-failure.** The existing `writeMcpEntry` already implements the correct shape: read `.mcp.json` (treat `ENOENT` as an empty `{}`), parse JSON, check `"aide" in servers || "aidemd-mcp" in servers` (matching the contract `wireMcp/index.ts` enforces at line 47), and if neither key is present, spread the existing `mcpServers` into a new object with `aide: mcpEntry()` appended. Serialize with 2-space indent and a trailing newline, write. If the file exists but fails `JSON.parse`, throw a clear error that names the file and instructs the user to fix the syntax — the CLI's outer `try/catch` converts the throw into a stderr error line and exits non-zero without writing a byte. This directly guards against the "clobber a malformed config" and "destroy existing entries" undesired outcomes and lets the user's other MCP servers (`github`, custom servers, a user-managed `obsidian` entry from their own setup) survive every run untouched.

**Per-file failures roll into the warning, never abort the run.** The `outcomes.undesired` contract is explicit: an unwritable agent file must not prevent the methodology stub, slash commands, MCP merge, and launcher from being installed. Each per-artifact helper returns a result object rather than throwing for routine failures (missing canonical content, EACCES on a single write, etc.); the orchestrator logs the failure line, appends the artifact to the skipped/failed list for the warning, and continues with the next artifact. The only conditions that abort the whole run are malformed `.mcp.json` (because continuing would write the MCP entry into a corrupted file tree) and unrecoverable process-level failures (uncaught exceptions from the Node runtime itself). This decision flows directly from the "unwritable per-file failure must not cascade" item in the undesired outcomes.

**The warning block is a loud ASCII-bordered region printed once after every per-file line, never inline with them.** The frontmatter calls the warning "hard-to-miss" and "unmissable" — the per-file log uses `[created]`/`[exists]` status prefixes with no decoration, so the warning has to break that visual rhythm to register. The layout is a rectangular box drawn with heavy ASCII borders (`=`/`|` or `█`, not the light `─`/`│` used by some TUIs), an uppercase heading like `SETUP INCOMPLETE — ACTION REQUIRED`, a numbered list of every skipped file path (one per line, prefixed with the reason: "already present" vs "write failed: <error>"), and a numbered list of every deferred category — each entry carrying its own follow-up surface inline (brain wiring: open Claude Code and run `/aide`, the orchestrator prompts for the vault path, scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide`, and tells you to run sync; IDE: re-run the CLI with `--ide <choice>`). There is no single closing instruction line naming one command, because the deferred categories now route to different surfaces. The block goes after the last per-file line and before the exit so the user's eye lands on it as the final terminal state. Reserving ASCII bordering for this one block keeps the signal: the per-file log uses bare status prefixes, and the loud region is the only place the user's scan catches. This is the direct counter to the "quiet warning inline with log lines" undesired outcome.

**Warning suppression has one exact condition: zero skips and zero deferrals.** A fully installed project where every artifact is `[exists]` and no inherent-decision category remains unresolved prints the plain `Already set up.` line — no border, no warning. A cold-start install that wrote every artifact successfully but still needs to hand off brain/IDE decisions to the per-category follow-up surfaces must print the warning, because "deferred categories" is non-empty by definition on any first run. The CLI prints `Already set up.` only on a run where nothing is skipped and nothing is deferred; what changes is that any skipped or failed artifact, regardless of how many artifacts succeeded, flips the output to the warning-block form. An empty warning block printed over an all-clean run is the specific failure mode the undesired outcome "warning printed even when there is nothing to warn about" names.

**Per-file log is terse and color-free.** One line per artifact, status prefix first (`[created]` or `[exists]`; `[failed]` on a per-file write failure that rolls into the warning), host-relative path next, short reason last. No colors, no spinners, no progress bars, no ASCII art in the per-file lines — the loud ASCII is reserved for the final warning so it stands out against this flat log. This matches the format the current `runInit` in `src/cli/init/index.ts` already uses (`write(\`[${result.status}] ${LABEL} — ${result.message}\`)`) and extends it across the full artifact set with no visual change per line.

**Framework targeting is Claude Code only, at fixed paths.** The CLI writes to `.claude/commands/aide/` for slash commands, `.claude/agents/aide/` for pipeline agents, `.claude/skills/` for skill templates, `.mcp.json` for MCP config, `CLAUDE.md` (or the existing host config the stub discovers) for the methodology pointer, `.aide/docs/` for the methodology hub, and `.aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs` for the launcher. Multi-framework detection (Cursor, Windsurf, Zed, Copilot) is out of scope at cold-start: the CLI runs before any agent session exists, and there is no user to confirm a detected framework choice. Any other framework's user opens their framework and `aide_init` handles it from there — which is the right place for framework choice, because it's an inherent decision the same way brain and IDE are. This narrows the CLI's contract to exactly the surface a Claude Code cold start needs.

**The CLI remains a single async IIFE with a tiny, hand-parsed flag surface.** The entry shape from `src/cli/init/index.ts` — sequential `await` calls per artifact, `write(line)` for every per-file status, final summary line — scales to the full canonical-artifact set without requiring Commander, Yargs, or any argument-parsing framework. The recognized flags are `--help`, `--vault-path <path>` (also `--vault-path=<path>`), and `--ide <choice>` — each parsed inline by a small reader function with no third-party dependency. Zero prompts, regardless of which flags were supplied. The `npx` invocation is itself the opt-in; re-confirming with a `y/N` gate is the exact friction the frontmatter's undesired outcome rules out.

## Good examples

A developer runs the installer in a cold project with no `.mcp.json` and no `.claude/` tree yet. The per-file log streams as each artifact is placed, then a warning block announces the work the CLI could not do:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
[created] .aide/docs/index.md — methodology hub index
[created] .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — canonical AIDE spec
[created] .aide/docs/aide-template.md — canonical AIDE template
[created] .aide/docs/progressive-disclosure.md — canonical progressive-disclosure doc
[created] .aide/docs/agent-readable-code.md — canonical agent-readable-code doc
[created] .aide/docs/automated-qa.md — canonical automated-qa doc
[created] .aide/docs/plan-aide.md — canonical plan.aide doc
