[exists] .claude/commands/aide/spec.md — already present
[created] .claude/commands/aide/synthesize.md — /aide:synthesize
[created] .claude/commands/aide/plan.md — /aide:plan
[created] .claude/commands/aide/build.md — /aide:build
[created] .claude/commands/aide/qa.md — /aide:qa
[created] .claude/commands/aide/fix.md — /aide:fix
[created] .claude/commands/aide/upgrade.md — /aide:upgrade
[created] .claude/commands/aide/update-playbook.md — /aide:update-playbook
[created] .claude/commands/aide/refactor.md — /aide:refactor
[created] .claude/commands/aide/align.md — /aide:align
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-spec-writer.md — pipeline agent
...
[created] .mcp.json — aide MCP server entry (merged with 1 existing server)
[created] .aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs — aide-tree launcher

============================================================
  SETUP INCOMPLETE — ACTION REQUIRED
============================================================

  The installer placed every file it could safely write, but
  some files already existed (skipped, never overwritten) and
  some setup steps require decisions only you can make.

  Skipped files (already on disk or write failed — not overwritten):
    1. CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub already present
    2. .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — already present
    3. .aide/docs/progressive-disclosure.md — already present
    4. .claude/commands/aide/research.md — already present
    5. .claude/commands/aide/spec.md — already present

  Deferred (each item names how to finish it):
    1. Brain wiring (.aide/config/brain.aide + derived brain MCP entry) — open Claude Code and run /aide; the orchestrator will prompt for the vault path, scaffold .aide/config/brain.aide, and tell you to run npx aidemd-mcp sync (which writes the derived brain entry into .mcp.json)
    2. IDE configuration — re-run: npx aidemd-mcp init --ide <choice>

============================================================
```

The existing `github` MCP server entry survives untouched. The five pre-existing files are explicitly named in the warning so the user can investigate whether their on-disk versions are current.

A developer runs the installer a second time on a project where every canonical artifact is already present:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[exists] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub already present
[exists] .aide/docs/index.md — already present
[exists] .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — already present
...
[exists] .mcp.json — aide server already configured
[exists] .aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs — aide-tree launcher already present

Already set up.
```

No files were modified and no categories remained deferred in this run (brain wiring was resolved in a prior `--vault-path` run that scaffolded `.aide/config/brain.aide` and merged the derived `brain` MCP entry; IDE was resolved in a prior `--ide` run), so the warning block is suppressed and the plain completion line is printed instead. Exit code 0. This is the idempotency contract the frontmatter requires. Note that even on this fully-clean rerun, the CLI did not touch `.aide/config/brain.aide` — that file is user-owned the moment it lands and stays out of the install path forever.

A developer runs the installer with `--vault-path` on a cold project. The CLI scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` from the canonical Obsidian template and derives the `brain` MCP entry from it:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init --vault-path D:/notes/my-vault
[created] .aide/config/brain.aide — Brain config (Obsidian default)
[created] .mcp.json — aide and brain MCP server entries
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
... (per-file log continues for every shipped artifact)

============================================================
  SETUP INCOMPLETE — ACTION REQUIRED
============================================================

  Deferred (each item names how to finish it):
    1. IDE configuration — re-run: npx aidemd-mcp init --ide <choice>

============================================================
```

The scaffolded `.aide/config/brain.aide` looks like this — frontmatter contains exactly `name` and `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`, with the supplied vault path inlined byte-for-byte in `args` (no `${rootPath}` substitution surface in the default scaffold), and the prose body the agent reads verbatim:

```
---
name: obsidian
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    - "D:/notes/my-vault"
---

## Prose

Your brain is an Obsidian vault. Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by
their vault-relative path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries
across every note in the vault. (...remainder of the canonical hand-written
prose body...)
```

The corresponding `.mcp.json` `brain` entry is `{ command: "npx", args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/notes/my-vault"] }` — derived from the scaffolded file via `parseBrainAideFromString` + `interpolateArgs` (with no placeholders to interpolate in the default), never from a hardcoded launcher constant. On every subsequent run, `.aide/config/brain.aide` reports `[exists]` and the CLI does not touch it — even if the user has retargeted the vault path, swapped the launcher to a different command, or rewritten the prose body.

A developer runs the installer against a project whose `.mcp.json` has a trailing comma from a hand-edit mistake:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
Error: .mcp.json exists but contains invalid JSON. Fix the syntax error and re-run.
```

Exit code 1. No files were written. Not even the methodology stub, the doc hub, the commands, the agents, or the launcher — the CLI aborts the whole run because continuing would write into a project whose MCP wiring is already broken. The user fixes the syntax and re-runs; the second run installs cleanly.

## Bad examples

The CLI silently replaces the user's existing `.mcp.json`:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] .mcp.json — aide MCP server entry
...
Done. Open Claude Code and run /aide:upgrade to reconcile any existing files.
```

The user had `github`, a legacy `obsidian` key from a pre-migration install, and two custom MCP servers configured. All of them are gone, overwritten by a fresh file containing only the `aide` entry. The CLI decided the on-disk config was stale and assumed it could replace it — the exact failure mode the frontmatter's "never overwrites" contract exists to prevent. (The legacy `obsidian` key, in particular, must survive untouched here — the install service's `provisionBrain` is the one place migration to the fixed `brain` key happens, never the CLI silently.) The user has no way of knowing until their other MCP servers stop appearing in Claude Code.

The CLI finishes with files skipped but never surfaces the skip list:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] .aide/docs/aide-template.md — canonical AIDE template
[exists] .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — already present
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-architect.md — pipeline agent
[exists] .claude/agents/aide/aide-spec-writer.md — already present
...
Done.
```

Three files were skipped across methodology, agents, and commands. The user sees `[exists]` markers in the stream but no summary — scrolling back through 35 lines to reconstruct the skip list is the kind of work the warning block is supposed to do for them. They walk away thinking the install is complete, then trip on an outdated agent file a week later and have no record of which files the CLI did not write.

The CLI surfaces skips inline with the log instead of as a loud block:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] .aide/docs/aide-template.md — canonical AIDE template
[exists] .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — already present
WARNING: skipped because file exists
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-architect.md — pipeline agent
[exists] .claude/agents/aide/aide-spec-writer.md — already present
WARNING: skipped because file exists
...
Done. Open Claude Code and run /aide:upgrade to reconcile skipped files.
```

The warnings are structurally inline with the `[created]`/`[exists]` lines, using the same font weight, the same indentation, and the same width as the rest of the log. A user skimming the output reads every line as routine status and never registers that the install is incomplete. This is the "quiet warning" failure mode the undesired outcomes explicitly name — the warning has to break the log's visual rhythm with borders and uppercase heading to register.

The CLI aborts the entire run on a single file's write failure:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
[created] .aide/docs/index.md — methodology hub index
Error: EACCES: permission denied, open '.aide/docs/aide-spec.md'
```

Exit code 1. The methodology stub made it in, nothing else did — not the rest of the doc hub, not the slash commands, not the agents, not the MCP entry, not the launcher. The user has to chase down the permission issue and re-run before they can open Claude Code and start using `/aide`. A single per-file failure must roll into the warning alongside skipped files and let the rest of the install continue.

The CLI attempts to guess a brain vault path:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] .mcp.json — aide and brain MCP server entries
Detected brain vault at ../my-brain — wired brain MCP server
Done.
```

The user's sibling `../my-brain` directory is actually their spouse's personal journal vault, not an AIDE brain. The CLI discovered it from a filesystem scan, wired the `brain` entry to point at it, and silently committed the user's pipeline to writing research notes into a vault they never consented to. Brain vault is an inherent decision — the CLI has no business resolving it, and the frontmatter explicitly forbids guessing from a sibling folder, env var, or platform default.

The CLI prompts the user mid-run:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
The .aide/docs/ hub will contain the canonical methodology files. Continue? [y/N]
```

The happy path must be zero-interaction. The `npx` invocation is the opt-in; a confirmation prompt adds friction to a command whose whole purpose is to be runnable unattended, and it violates the frontmatter contract that the CLI cannot prompt because there is no UI for it.

## References

- `src/service/install/.aide` (install service) -- confirms the shared overarching semantic: prompts are reserved for overwrites and inherent decisions, silent-on-create is the fast-path rule. The CLI adaptation is skip-or-abort because terminal context has no UI to prompt with — the content of this spec's "skip-on-exists as only safe branch" strategy paragraph.
- `src/.aide` -- parent spec; source of the "two delivery channels" frame and the idempotency-as-invariant contract that carries down into the CLI's repeated-run behavior and the `Already set up.` suppression rule.
- `src/cli/init/index.ts` -- current `runInit` shape: sequential per-artifact `await`, status-prefixed log lines, final completion line. The strategy extends this shape to the full canonical-artifact set without changing the log format.
- `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.ts` -- the CLI-local thin wrapper around the shared `cli/shared/writeMcpEntry` helper. Adds CLI-specific concerns (the `WriteMcpEntryResult` shape, the message composition, the aide entry build, and the brain-entry-derived-from-`.aide/config/brain.aide` step) on top of the shared mutation helper.
- `src/service/install/wireMcp/index.ts` -- `mcpEntry()` returns the platform-branching `aide` server entry the CLI writes verbatim; the dual-key check is the contract `writeMcpEntry` matches so subsequent `aide_init` MCP steps report `exists` instead of adding a duplicate. (Moved from `src/tools/init/` during the install-service refactor.)
- `src/service/install/initContent/index.ts` -- `readCanonicalDoc` is the content-resolution primitive; `DOC_PATHS` is the canonical-name-to-path registry; `listMethodologyDocs`/`listAgents`/`listSkills` are the canonical enumerations the CLI iterates to install each category. The strategy reuses these directly, bypassing the planner helpers built on top of them.
- `src/service/install/installMethodologyDocs/index.ts`, `src/service/install/installAgents/index.ts`, `src/service/install/installSkills/index.ts`, `src/service/install/scaffoldCommands/index.ts` -- planner helpers that return `InitStep[]` for agent-driven application. The CLI also uses these planners (filtering `would-create` only) so the partition is the apply-side enforcement of skip-on-exists.
- `src/service/install/writeMethodology/index.ts` -- source of `composeStub` and the `METHODOLOGY_MARKER` constant via `getMethodologyMarker`; the CLI uses both to compose the pointer stub and detect the marker for skip-on-exists on the host config file.
- `src/service/install/scaffoldCommands/index.ts` -- `COMMANDS` registry enumerates the pipeline slash commands the CLI installs; the `hostPath` field defines the `.claude/commands/aide/<phase>.md` layout.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts` -- single source of canonical Obsidian `brain.aide` bytes. The CLI imports this when `--vault-path` is supplied and writes the result to `.aide/config/brain.aide`; the launcher shape (`@bitbonsai/mcpvault`) and the platform-branching `command`/`args` shape live here and nowhere else in the CLI.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` -- `parseBrainAideFromString` and `interpolateArgs` are the consumers the CLI uses to derive the `brain` MCP entry from the scaffolded (or pre-existing user-edited) `.aide/config/brain.aide`.
- `src/cli/shared/writeMcpEntry/index.ts` -- shared `.mcp.json` mutation helper that both `cli/init` and `cli/sync` route through; carries the dual-key existence check, the additive merge semantics, and the abort-on-malformed-JSON contract.
- `.aide/docs/aide-template.md` -- canonical body-section contract for the spec itself: Context explains why the module exists, Strategy distills decisions with inline citations, Good/Bad examples are real output, References logs what was actually used during synthesis.
- `.aide/docs/progressive-disclosure.md` and `.aide/docs/aide-spec.md` -- methodology docs that establish the "read specs before code" and "stub-in-config, hub-on-disk" patterns the pointer-stub + doc-hub install enacts at the delivery layer.
